      "headline": "Kenna's Dilemma: The Right\u2014and Wrong\u2014Way to Ask People What They Want",
      "keyInsight": "Asking customers what they want sounds like standard business discipline, but market surveys often kill real innovation. When an artist named Kenna shared his boundary-pushing music, industry legends loved it.\n\nYet ordinary focus groups ranked it dead last. Why?\n\nBecause the human brain systematically confuses the unfamiliar with the bad. When asked to evaluate something genuinely novel, consumers reach for familiar yardsticks.\n\nMarket research rewards derivative, comfortable mediocrity and penalizes breakthrough ideas. If you judge breakthrough products through vanilla consumer polls, you will systematically discard your most valuable innovations.\n\nTrue category-defining pioneers do not rely on focus group approval. They trust expert taste, build high-conviction prototypes, and understand that groundbreaking craft requires time to reshape customer preferences.",
      "content": "A talented musician named Kenna created an extraordinary sound blending rock, funk, and electronic beats. Record producers and top music tastemakers were completely blown away. They thought he was the next global superstar.\n\nThen the record label ran his songs through mainstream market test screenings. Ordinary listeners in focus groups hated it. Radio programmers refused to air his tracks.\n\nThe market research concluded the music was unlistenable, despite top critics calling it genius.\n\nKenna hit an invisible wall called the novelty trap. When people encounter an entirely new category, their immediate gut reaction is discomfort. Standard consumer surveys do not measure lasting brilliance; they measure superficial familiarity.\n\nA bland pop song scores high immediately because it mimics familiar radio hits. A radical sound demands new neural pathways, so focus groups reject it on the spot.\n\nConsider the Aeron office chair when Herman Miller first tested it. People in focus groups called it an ugly alien contraption made of weird mesh. Traditional executives wanted to cancel the project and stick to plush leather armchairs.\n\nBut the designers had deep conviction in the ergonomic science. They backed the bold design, shipped it to actual offices, and watched it become the most iconic, best-selling luxury task chair in history.\n\nMarket research is great for tweaking existing products by small percentages. It is terrible for evaluating bold strategic leaps. If you ask people to vote on the unfamiliar, they will always vote for the safe copycat.\n\nBreakthrough leadership requires trusting domain expertise and having the stomach to back genuine originality before the crowd catches up.",
      "keyTakeaway": "Consumers mistake unfamiliarity for low quality, meaning focus groups will kill your most original, high-upside breakthroughs.",
      "audioSnippetText": "Market research measures familiarity rather than true long-term value. Novel breakthroughs make people uncomfortable before they become category-defining hits.",
